SUMMARY:Applied Micro – Simon Chang (University of Western Australia) –
  When Brides Are Scarce: Marriage Competition and Sons’ Schooling in Chi
 na
LOCATION:Room 404\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Simon Chang: “When Brides Are Scarce: Marriage Competition an
 d Sons’ Schooling in China”\nhttps://sites.google.com/site/simonchang1
 975/\n\nZoom: https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/84741930353?pwd=1TDakj4y62yHU6dWbk
 6ZMFehNtmGMq.1&from=addon\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKu
 rX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nIn China\, the groom’s family is expected to pro
 vide much of the wealth a couple needs to marry. Whatever a family gives u
 p to keep a son in school\, whether money spent on schooling or the earnin
 gs he forgoes\, it will not have when he marries. We show that competition
  for brides raises this opportunity cost. In our model\, families cannot b
 orrow against a son’s future earnings\, so when men outnumber women\, mo
 ney in hand at marriage becomes more valuable\, most of all for families o
 f modest means. Using the China Family Panel Studies and an instrument tha
 t interacts the diffusion of prenatal ultrasound with historical son prefe
 rence across provinces\, we find that one additional man per 100 women low
 ers boys’ junior-high completion by 6.4 percentage points. The effect on
  girls is small and statistically insignificant\, there is no significant 
 effect at senior high or college\, and the effect appears only in cohorts 
 born after ultrasound spread. The decline is concentrated among rural boys
  and\, although imprecisely estimated\, is largest among families of modes
 t means\, as the model predicts. Marriage-market competition can discourag
 e the accumulation of human capital by raising the value of wealth at marr
 iage.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/84741930353?pwd=1TDakj4y62yHU6dWbk6ZMFehNtmGM
 q.1&from=addon
END:VEVENT
