SUMMARY:Econometrics – Qingliang Fan (CUHK) – Utility-Maximizing Ensemb
 les of Characteristic-Based Portfolio Policies
LOCATION:Room 404\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Qingliang Fan: “Utility-Maximizing Ensembles of Characteristi
 c-Based Portfolio Policies”\nhttps://www.econ.cuhk.edu.hk/en/about/peopl
 e/faculty/regular-faculty-members/prof-fan-qingliang/\n\nZoom: https://uni
 melb.zoom.us/j/85966225829?pwd=g6hOdHyvYB0qta0aFzGbWOgczJ1Qzr.1\n\nGoogle 
 Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nAgents obser
 ving panel data on outcomes and characteristics select allocation decision
 s to maximize expected utility of aggregate outcomes. We develop a Charact
 eristics-based Utility-maximizing Ensemble Learning (CUEL) framework to ad
 dress model uncertainty\, feature complexity\, and implementation costs in
  general utility maximization problems. Instead of selecting a single mode
 l\, CUEL combines candidate models that target the exact same objective as
  the final ensemble: maximizing expected utility subject to explicit penal
 ties. We establish the asymptotic optimality of the CUEL estimator and the
  consistency of the combination weights for out-of-sample prediction risk\
 , even when all candidates are misspecified. In high-dimensional portfolio
  applications directly targeting out-of-sample (OOS) utility functions\, C
 UEL significantly outperforms unrestricted estimation\, sparse selection\,
  naive averaging\, and machine learning benchmarks after accounting for tr
 ansaction costs. When the target is the out-of-sample Sharpe ratio\, CUEL 
 shows similar competence as well. It achieves OOS optimality by aggregatin
 g information from a broad set of characteristics rather than selecting a 
 sparse subset\, leveraging small but economically meaningful signals in a 
 stable manner\, including under gross-exposure constraints for practical i
 mplementation. Our method provides clear economic explanations of why some
  characteristics play significant roles under different utility targets an
 d risk preferences.
URL:https://unimelb.zoom.us/j/85966225829?pwd=g6hOdHyvYB0qta0aFzGbWOgczJ1Qz
 r.1
END:VEVENT
