SUMMARY:Economic Theory – Arthur Campbell (Monash) – The Polarization P
 aradox: Why More Connections Can Divide Us
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3062
DESCRIPTION:Arthur Campbell: “The Polarization Paradox: Why More Connecti
 ons Can Divide Us”\nhttps://sites.google.com/site/arthurcampbellecon/\n\
 nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRACT:\nWe d
 evelop a simple model of content filtering - the tendency of individuals t
 o selectively forward information that aligns with their ideological prefe
 rences - to study how network structure shapes the distribution of politic
 al content. In our framework\, individuals and content are horizontally di
 fferentiated into three types (left\, middle\, right). We show that conten
 t filtering can amplify either centrist or extreme content and may lead to
  exclusively centrist content (full moderation) or exclusively extreme con
 tent (full polarization). The outcome depends on the interaction between t
 wo forces: a preference advantage\, driven by the relative prevalence of t
 ypes in the population\, and a pairwise comparison advantage\, which syste
 matically favors centrist content. Network density plays a critical role. 
 Sparse networks yield moderation\, even when extreme types dominate the po
 pulation\, whereas dense networks replicate the population's type distribu
 tion. Intermediate densities generate non--monotonic comparative statics\,
  including sharp transitions between moderation and polarization. Our pape
 r contributes to the debate on polarization by identifying a new theoretic
 al channel---content filtering---that complements belief/opinion--based ex
 planations.
END:VEVENT
