SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Yunjie Shi (University of Auckland) – W
 aiting and Preemption in Public Good Contributions
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3062
DESCRIPTION:Yunjie Shi: “Waiting and Preemption in Public Good Contributi
 ons”\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABSTRA
 CT:\nWe study the private provision of a threshold public good by two play
 ers who learn its quality from private signals arriving over time. The fir
 st contribution plays two strategic roles: its timing conveys information 
 about whether the first mover has received good news\, while its amount al
 locates the funding burden between the first mover and the follower. Takin
 g the first-contribution amount as exogenous\, we characterize the trade-o
 ff between the informational value of waiting and the incentive to secure 
 provision. This tension gives rise to three distinct equilibrium patterns:
  separation\, in which only players with good news initiate\; preemption\,
  in which uninformed players race to contribute at the outset\; and poolin
 g\, in which players with good news wait until the same contribution becom
 es sufficiently credible to induce an uninformed follower to complete the 
 project\, while uninformed players also initiate with positive probability
  at that time. Finally\, we show that the first-contribution amount can se
 rve as a design instrument. By reallocating the funding burden across cont
 ribution positions\, a fundraiser can implement the welfare-maximizing all
 ocation among the equilibrium paths we characterize: immediate provision w
 hen delay is sufficiently costly\, and provision after good news when lear
 ning is sufficiently valuable\, thereby attaining the single-decision-make
 r welfare benchmark.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
